  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 735 EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near normal temperatures and isolated to widely \nscattered afternoon/evening showers and storms are expected to \ncontinue through the weekend. \n\nEvening wx analysis shows a weakening upper level trough over \nthe southern Appalachians, with southerly flow aloft over the \nMid- Atlantic. Only a few isolated showers and storms have\nappeared on radar (mainly across the Piedmont and NE NC) thanks\nto height rises from the building ridge offshore and no \nboundaries/well defined forcing mechanism nearby. Convection\nshould continue until around sunset (given the loss of daytime\nheating), though the vast majority of the area will stay dry. \nFlash flooding appears unlikely at this time. However, cannot\ncompletely rule out very localized issues if a shower/storm \nhits one of the locations which received significant rainfall \nyesterday. The drying trend continues on Thursday with even more\nheight rises as the ridge offshore builds to the west. There is\nonly a slight chance for an isolated shower or storm during the\naftn/evening (mainly in the Piedmont). With lower storm \nchances, temps rebound to the upper 80s-lower 90s on Thursday \nwith upper 90s- low 100s heat indices. Similar conditions are \nexpected Friday into the weekend with some slight height falls \nover the local area as the upper ridge offshore retreats a bit \nto the SE and a series of weak shortwaves pass by to our N/NW. \nThis will lead to slightly greater chances for diurnally driven \nshowers or storms on Friday and Saturday across western and \nnorthern portions of the area but PoPs generally remain aob 30%.\nA few storms are possible on Sunday, with the highest precip \nchances (still only 20-30%) shifting to SE VA/NE NC. Seasonable \nheat and humidity will continue through the weekend, with heat \nindices in the upper 90s-low 100s each day.",
